{
  "dom_id": "question-129774",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "Find the value of ${a}_{5},$ the fifth nonzero term of the alternating series $S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${a}_{5}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the terms of our series $S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}$ are the coefficients of the Maclaurin series for $g(x).$\n\nPreviously, we found the fourth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x):$\n\n$$\nf(x)≈{P}_{4}(x)=2-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}\n$$\n\nTo find the ${x}^{5}$ term of the Maclaurin series of $g(x),$ we simply need to integrate the ${x}^{4}$ term of ${P}_{4}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{8}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{t}^{5}\\,{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${a}_{5}$ is the coefficient of $\\frac{1}{40}{x}^{5},$ that is, ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{40}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
